# Templates configure; they do not make the app “that client”

Canonical: `docs/CLIENT_TEMPLATES_AND_SETUP.md`. Read it **before**
implementing UI, onboarding, Settings toggles, or “this client needs…”
work in any Kumkaj app (Moklanjo, Khaynjo, Kirano, Tabiyat).

## Fact

- Each app is a **concrete trade product** (see `generic-concrete.mdc`) —
  courier desk, food floor, kirana, pharmacy — **not** a shared Kumkaj core.
- Inside that product, **client / app templates and Settings** are how
  different shops of the **same** business get different chrome: hide
  Kitchen when counter and kitchen are adjacent, cash-only pay chips,
  no tax face, no Floor, etc.
- A client engagement means **configure and hide**, not fork the app into
  a one-client binary or delete capability other shops still need.

## Do

- Implement capability once in the product; expose show/hide and defaults
  via Settings, Team rights, and template `shop_defaults` / packs.
- Prefer progressive depth: unused surfaces stay out of the way until
  enabled.
- For multi-person rush / status boards, follow the product model in
  `docs/CLIENT_TEMPLATES_AND_SETUP.md` §3 (Khaynjo Floor/Parcel vs
  Moklanjo Next booking + Ops) — do not invent the sibling’s board.

## Do not

- Hardcode one client’s layout, pay mix, or hidden peers as the only
  permanent face of the app.
- Remove Floor / Kitchen / Account booking / COD / etc. “because this
  client never uses them” without a Settings/template path to bring
  them back.
- Treat “concrete product” as permission to bake one shop’s setup into
  code forever.
- Assume every food shop needs full KDS chrome, or every courier desk
  needs the same Ops depth on screen day one.
